<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>biotechnology-applications</title></head><body><h1>biotechnology-applications</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Applications in agriculture and medicine</li></ul><h2>Applications in agriculture and medicine</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>### 1. Agricultural Applications</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>- **Bt Cotton**: Contains Bacillus thuringiensis gene for insect resistance</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>- **Bt Corn**: Pest-resistant corn expressing Bt toxin</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>- **Golden Rice**: Enhanced with vitamin A (beta-carotene)</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>- **Herbicide-resistant crops**: Tolerant to specific herbicides</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>- **Delayed ripening tomatoes**: Flavr Savr tomato</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>- **Drought-resistant crops**: Modified for water stress tolerance</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>### 2. Medical Applications</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>- **Insulin**: Produced by E. coli using rDNA technology (replaced animal insulin)</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>- **Growth Hormone**: For treating dwarfism</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>- **Vaccines**: Hepatitis B vaccine (yeast expression system)</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>- **Gene Therapy**: Treating genetic disorders by replacing faulty genes</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>- **Monoclonal Antibodies**: Cancer treatment, diagnostic tests</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>- **CRISPR-Cas9**: Gene editing for therapeutic purposes</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>### 3. Transgenic Animals</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>- **GloFish**: Fluorescent zebrafish (research)</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>- **Transgenic mice**: Disease models (cancer, Alzheimer's)</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>- **Pharming**: Animals producing pharmaceutical proteins in milk</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>- **RoboCow**: Producing human proteins in milk</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>### 4. Biopiracy and Patents</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>- **Biopiracy**: Unauthorized commercial use of biological resources</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>- **Basmati rice case**: US patent on Indian basmati (later revoked)</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>- **Neem case**: EU patent on neem derivatives (revoked)</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>- **Wheat case**: US patent on Indian wheat (revoked)</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>- **Protected Biological Resources**: Nepal has traditional knowledge databases</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>### 5. Bioremediation</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>- Using microbes to clean up environmental pollutants</td></tr><tr><td>28</td><td>- Oil-spill cleanup: Pseudomonas putida degrades hydrocarbons</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>- Heavy metal removal: Bioaccumulation by certain microbes</td></tr><tr><td>30</td><td>### 6. Entrance Exam Focus</td></tr><tr><td>31</td><td>Applications of Bt crops; Insulin production; Biopiracy examples.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Agricultural applications: Bt cotton, Bt corn, Golden Rice, herbicide-resistant crops, delayed-ripening tomato (Flavr Savr) and drought-resistant crops.</td></tr><tr><td>Medical applications: insulin, growth hormone for dwarfism, hepatitis B vaccine made in a yeast expression system, gene therapy, monoclonal antibodies and CRISPR-Cas9.</td></tr><tr><td>Flavr Savr, engineered to delay softening of tomatoes, was the first commercial GM food approved for sale, in 1994 in the United States.</td></tr><tr><td>CRISPR-Cas9 is a gene-editing tool in which a guide RNA directs a nuclease to a chosen DNA sequence, so that sequence can then be changed precisely.</td></tr><tr><td>Transgenic animals: GloFish (fluorescent zebrafish, a novelty and research organism), transgenic mice as disease models, and pharming animals that secrete therapeutic proteins in milk.</td></tr><tr><td>Bioremediation uses microbes such as Pseudomonas putida to degrade oil hydrocarbons, while other organisms bioaccumulate or biosorb heavy metals.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>x GM means genetically modified. x GWO (genetically wrought organism) is NOT the same term.</td></tr><tr><td>x All transgenic animals are harmful. x Many provide medical benefits.</td></tr><tr><td>x Biopiracy is legal. x It's the unauthorized exploitation of indigenous knowledge.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Biotechnology applies the use of living organisms and their products to make or modify useful products, and in modern practice this means recombinant DNA technology. In agriculture, Bt crops carrying Bacillus thuringiensis genes give insect resistance, Golden Rice supplies provitamin A, and herbicide-resistant, delayed-ripening and drought-tolerant varieties raise yields and food quality. In medicine, recombinant DNA gives human insulin, growth hormone and hepatitis B vaccine, while gene therapy, monoclonal antibodies and CRISPR-Cas9 treat and diagnose disease at the genetic level. Transgenic animals serve as disease models and as factories producing therapeutic proteins, and the same tools clean up pollution through bioremediation, although the unauthorised use of indigenous knowledge raises the ethical problem of biopiracy.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Applications in agriculture and medicine</td><td>Biotechnology applies the use of living organisms and their products to make or modify useful products, and in modern practice this means recombinant DNA technology. In agriculture, Bt crops carrying Bacillus thuringiensis genes give insect resistance, Golden Rice supplies provitamin A, and herbicide-resistant, delayed-ripening and drought-tolerant varieties raise yields and food quality. In medicine, recombinant DNA gives human insulin, growth hormone and hepatitis B vaccine, while gene therapy, monoclonal antibodies and CRISPR-Cas9 treat and diagnose disease at the genetic level. Transgenic animals serve as disease models and as factories producing therapeutic proteins, and the same tools clean up pollution through bioremediation, although the unauthorised use of indigenous knowledge raises the ethical problem of biopiracy.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Biotechnology Applications.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>PCR amplifies a selected DNA region and gel electrophoresis separates fragments by size. These tools support research and diagnosis, but results depend on controls and the specific question asked.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>Detection of genetic material alone does not necessarily show that an organism is alive or caused symptoms. For genome editing, consider off-target changes, mosaicism, delivery to the intended cells and the distinction between somatic and heritable changes.</p><h3>Open research question — Precision and safety in genome editing</h3><p>Researchers continue to improve how genome edits are delivered and assessed. Off-target changes, mosaicism, intended tissue, and—in heritable editing—effects on future generations are distinct scientific and ethical considerations.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>For “Biotechnology Applications”, identify one assumption in the model, explain what observation would test it, and state what evidence could distinguish the accepted account from the open question “Precision and safety in genome editing”.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/17-1-biotechnology">OpenStax Biology: biotechnology</a></li><li><a href="https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/policy-issues/Genome-Editing/ethical-concerns">NHGRI: ethical concerns in genome editing</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>